---
import { repositoryUrl, site } from '@/data/site';
import LegalLayout from '@/layouts/legal.astro';
import { supportMailtoUrl } from '@/lib/urls';
---

<LegalLayout
  title={`Privacy Policy — ${site.name}`}
  description="Arkie collects nothing. What it stores, where it stores it, and what it connects to."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    Arkie collects no personal data and sends nothing to us. There is no account, no analytics, no
    telemetry and no crash reporting. The only machine Arkie connects to is the Counter-Strike 2
    server whose address you typed in.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <p>Nothing. Specifically, Arkie does not collect, log, transmit or process:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>personal information of any kind;</li>
    <li>usage analytics or product metrics;</li>
    <li>telemetry or crash reports;</li>
    <li>
      any IP address other than the ones you enter yourself in order to reach your own servers.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Arkie stores, and where</h2>
  <p>
    Saved servers — the name, address, port and RCON password — are written to your computer's
    local application storage so Arkie can reconnect without asking you again. The same goes for
    commands you save, and for your theme and privacy-mode preferences.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of it is uploaded, synchronised or backed up by Arkie. It stays on the machine you put it
    on, and it is removed when you delete the server or uninstall the app.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>That local storage is not encrypted, and RCON passwords are part of it.</strong> A
    password has to be readable for Arkie to send it. Treat the machine running Arkie the way you
    would treat the server's console.
  </p>

  <h2>Network connections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Arkie connects only to servers you explicitly configure, at the address and port you give.</li>
    <li>Every connection is one you started, and it goes straight to your server.</li>
    <li>Arkie does not talk to any server we run, to any third party, or to any analytics service.</li>
    <li>
      Source RCON is an unencrypted protocol. Your password and your commands travel to your server
      in the clear, the same as they would from any other RCON client. Over an untrusted network,
      put the connection inside a VPN or an SSH tunnel.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Third-party services</h2>
  <p>
    Arkie integrates with no third-party service and bundles no analytics, advertising or
    attribution SDK.
  </p>
  <p>
    Downloading Arkie is a separate matter: every installer is served by GitHub, which applies its
    own privacy policy to a download, and we receive nothing from it beyond the aggregate counts it
    shows publicly.
  </p>

  <h2>Verifying this yourself</h2>
  <p>
    Arkie is open source under the {site.license} licence. You do not have to take any of this on
    trust — <a href={repositoryUrl}>read the source</a> and confirm it.
  </p>

  <h2>Children's privacy</h2>
  <p>
    Arkie does not knowingly collect personal information from anyone, including children under the
    age of 13, because it does not collect personal information at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the updated version will be published here and in the repository, and
    the date at the top of this page will change with it.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy or about what Arkie does on your machine go to <a
      href={supportMailtoUrl}>{site.supportEmail}</a
    >, or to the <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/issues`}>issue tracker</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
